### What about overflow and money precision?

When bar heights represent financial amounts, use BigDecimal for exact arithmetic. Even with integer heights, the sum of trapped water across many bars can overflow an int. Using long for the running total is the minimum safeguard.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TrappingRainBigDecimal {
    public static BigDecimal trap(BigDecimal[] heights) {
        if (heights == null || heights.length < 3) return BigDecimal.ZERO;
        int left = 0, right = heights.length - 1;
        BigDecimal leftMax = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        BigDecimal rightMax = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        BigDecimal total = BigDecimal.ZERO;

        while (left < right) {
            if (heights[left].compareTo(heights[right]) <= 0) {
                if (heights[left].compareTo(leftMax) >= 0) {
                    leftMax = heights[left];
                } else {
                    total = total.add(leftMax.subtract(heights[left]));
                }
                left++;
            } else {
                if (heights[right].compareTo(rightMax) >= 0) {
                    rightMax = heights[right];
                } else {
                    total = total.add(rightMax.subtract(heights[right]));
                }
                right--;
            }
        }
        return total;
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most frequent mistake is using the wrong comparison direction. Processing the side with the larger boundary instead of the smaller one breaks the correctness invariant.

```java
// WRONG: processing the larger side
if (heights[left] > heights[right]) {  // Bug: should be <=
    // Processing left when right is smaller
    // The left side's water is NOT determined yet
}

// CORRECT: process the shorter side
if (heights[left] <= heights[right]) {
    // Process left: right side guarantees a taller wall
}
```

Another common mistake is forgetting the edge case of fewer than three bars, which causes an index out of bounds or a wrong non-zero result.

### When should you use this approach?

Use the two-pointer Trapping Rain Water approach when you need O(n) time with O(1) space for computing trapped quantities between peaks. It is a classic interview problem that tests the ability to maintain invariants with two converging pointers.

In a banking context, this applies to computing trapped liquidity in balance skylines, where dips between higher-balance days represent idle reserves that could not drain without breaching a wall. The constant-space two-pointer method is preferred over prefix arrays when the daily series spans years of data.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LiquidityReserveAnalytic {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Daily balance skyline for Rajesh's account over a week
        int[] dailyBalances = {50000, 20000, 35000, 15000, 60000, 30000, 45000};
        long trappedReserve = TrappingRainTwoPointer.trap(dailyBalances);
        System.out.println("Trapped idle reserve: " + trappedReserve);
    }
}
```

```text
Trapped idle reserve: 40000
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should be able to prove the invariant formally. At every step, everything to the left of the left pointer has been fully accounted for, everything to the right of the right pointer has been fully accounted for, and leftMax and rightMax are the true maxima of the processed-and-current regions on each side. Because you only commit water on the side whose boundary is guaranteed smaller, you never over-count or under-count.

The stack-based approach is another O(n) solution that processes bars differently, maintaining a stack of bar indices and computing horizontal layers of water when a taller bar arrives. It is useful for understanding but the two-pointer approach is simpler and uses less space.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers commonly ask these follow-ups after Trapping Rain Water:

- Can you solve it in O(1) space? (Yes, the two-pointer approach.)
- What if the bars are in 2D, a grid? (Trapping Rain Water II uses a priority queue BFS from the border.)
- Can you compute trapped water using a stack? (Yes, the monotonic stack approach computes horizontal layers.)
- What if bar heights are floating-point? (Use BigDecimal for exact arithmetic or accept floating-point precision limits.)
